---
import SectionHeading from './SectionHeading.astro';
---

<section id="pdf-to-excel" class="scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border bg-paper">
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-5 py-16 sm:px-6 sm:py-20 lg:px-8">
    <SectionHeading
      eyebrow="Convert PDF to Excel"
      title={[
        { text: 'A PDF-to-Excel tool that never uploads your file.' },
      ]}
      lede="Long story short: you drop a PDF in, you get an Excel or CSV file back, and your document never touches a server. The longer story — and the details that make that possible — follows below."
    />

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-x-12 gap-y-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="prose-section space-y-5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        <p>
          Most PDF-to-Excel converters work by shipping your document to a
          server, converting it there, and then — if the company is reputable —
          deleting it after an hour. The act of uploading is the whole point of
          those tools, because it is how they charge you: account walls, daily
          task limits, watermarked previews. This converter works the opposite
          way. A PDF that contains real text is opened and parsed directly in
          your browser tab, using your own computer's processor. The bytes are
          read from your disk, turned into a spreadsheet in memory, and
          discarded when you close the tab. There is no upload to wait for and
          nothing left behind.
        </p>
        <p>
          What that buys you, practically, is speed and privacy at once. A
          ten-page bank statement converts in seconds rather than after a
          queue, because the work happens locally. No waiting for "your file is
          being processed" screens. And because the document never travels, the
          everyday privacy bargain of online conversion tools — trust us, we
          delete after an hour — simply does not apply to text-based PDFs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Table extraction is the heart of the tool. When a PDF contains an
          actual table — an invoice listing, a transactions export, an
          inventory sheet — the converter detects the column positions from the
          text layer and rebuilds the grid rather than dumping a wall of words
          into a single cell. The preview shows the detected columns and rows
          before you commit to a download, so you can see whether the
          extraction matches the original before anything leaves your machine
          in file form.
        </p>
        <p>
          The best-fit documents are the ones that already have structure:
          bank and credit card statements, supplier invoices, expense lists,
          payroll excerpts, and export-style PDFs generated by accounting or
          CRM software. Those convert both accurately and fast, because their
          text layer is clean. It is also honest to say which files are harder.
          A form with multi-column instructions, a tightly packed magazine
          layout, or a table split awkwardly across a page boundary may need a
          little cleanup after conversion — the same is true on every converter
          in this category.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is also worth sizing against the free built-in options. Excel
          can import PDF data, but it routes the file to Microsoft's cloud and
          treats a table as unstructured text to guess at; Google Sheets and
          LibreOffice offer no native PDF import at all. A dedicated converter
          exists precisely because the generic spreadsheet tools are not good
          at reconstructing a PDF table. Here, the reconstruction is the
          entire product, and the preview lets you confirm it worked before you
          download anything.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-section space-y-5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        <p>
          The output side is two formats. Excel (.xlsx) gives you multiple
          sheets and a file that opens cleanly in Excel, LibreOffice, Google
          Sheets, and Numbers. CSV (.csv) gives you a plain, universally
          portable file for feeds, accounting software, and databases. The same
          extracted table becomes either, whichever your workflow needs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Word documents (.docx and .doc) and PowerPoint files (.pptx and
          .ppt) are covered too — a table inside a Word report or a data slide
          converts the same way, in your browser, with the same zero-upload
          behavior. File size is limited only by what your browser can handle,
          not by a server quota.
        </p>
        <p>
          Scanned PDFs and photos are the honest exception, and we say so
          plainly. A scan is a picture, and pictures have no text for a browser
          to read. For those files we switch to OCR — optical character
          recognition — which runs on a dedicated service and is deleted the
          moment recognition finishes. That is the one case where your file
          leaves your device, and it is the reason there is a daily allowance:
          ten conversions free every day, resetting at midnight UTC, covering
          native and scanned files alike. Every converted file is yours to
          download and forget.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no account required for a single conversion, no watermark
          stamped across your results, and no feature hidden behind a trial
          counter. Two formats in, three formats out, one clear allowance. If
          you convert documents a few times a month — receipts, statements,
          forms — the free tier covers it. If you convert every day, Pro
          removes the limit and adds batch processing. Either way, the file
          works the same way: on your hardware, under your control.
        </p>
        <p>
          One practical note: because native PDFs are parsed in the browser,
          the conversion keeps working even when the connection to the OCR
          service is slow or down — there is no server call to make. And since
          nothing is uploaded, there is no file to "clean up" afterward, no
          retention policy to wonder about, and no reason to trust anyone about
          deletion in the first place.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-4xl rounded-lg border border-primary/20 bg-primary-soft/50 px-6 py-5">
      <p class="text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        <strong class="font-medium text-ink">The one-line version:</strong> a
        free PDF-to-Excel and PDF-to-CSV converter — plus Word and PowerPoint —
        that does the work in your browser, stores nothing, and only reaches
        for an OCR service when a file is a scan.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>